The Line 1 extension to Barcelona is a rapid transit extension under construction in [[Sant Cristòfor]], [[Balboa]]. The project will extend [[Line 1 (Sant Cristòfor Metro)|Line 1]] of the [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor]] from [[Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal]] in [[Original (ciutat)|Original]] to [[Badia de la Via Verda station]] in [[Barcelona (Sant Cristòfor)|Barcelona]], where it will interchange with [[Line 5 (Sant Cristòfor Metro)|Line 5]]. The extension adds no new stations, instead creating a direct connection between two existing stations.

Construction began on 10 June 2026, with completion expected in June 2027 and opening scheduled for October 2027. The project has a revised budget of I$680 million, funded primarily through municipal bonds and a provincial transport grant, with a national government contribution pending Assembly approval. The project is highly controversial because it will compete with the [[Ferries de la Badia de Sant Cristòfor]], the historic ferry network that links the Barcelona peninsula with Original across the bay. The project includes a retraining programme for ferry workers in case ferry demand declines after the metro extension opens.

== Route ==

The extension will connect two existing stations:

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Station !! Lines !! Notes
|-
| [[Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal]] || Line 1, Line 2 || Eastern terminus of Lines 1 and 2
|-
| [[Badia de la Via Verda station]] || Line 5, Line 1 (2027) || Interchange with Line 5; serves Barcelona district
|}

The route runs approximately 2.8 kilometres from Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal to Badia de la Via Verda. The extension will allow passengers from the eastern terminus to reach Barcelona without transferring to Line 5 at intermediate stations, reducing travel time by approximately 12 minutes compared with the current routing. The Sant Cristòfor Metropolitan Transport Authority estimates that the extension will add about 200,000 passengers per day to Line 1 once it opens.

== Construction ==

The extension is being constructed using a combination of cut-and-cover works on the Original side, a new underground station box beneath Badia de la Via Verda, and immersed tube construction for the underwater section crossing the Badia de Sant Cristòfor. The project involves engineering challenges due to the proximity of existing infrastructure and the layered station boxes at Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal.

=== Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal modifications ===

At Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal, the project requires modifications to the existing station box. Tracks 5 and 6, which are used by Lines 1 and 2, will remain in service throughout most of the work, although some rush-hour trains will short-turn at [[Plaça dels Campions]] to manage load. A connection wall will be opened within the station box to link the existing terminus to the new tunnel.

The station box at the Central Terminal is complex: the Line 1 and Line 2 tracks (5 and 6) sit above a deeper level housing Lines 3 and 5, which run north-south. The new connection must thread through this layered infrastructure without disrupting the lower lines, requiring precision excavation and structural monitoring.

=== Immersed tube section ===

The most technically demanding section of the project is the crossing of the Badia de Sant Cristòfor. The extension will use immersed tube construction: precast concrete tunnel sections will be fabricated at the [[Port of Sant Cristòfor]], floated into position, and sunk onto a prepared trench on the bay floor. The tubes will lie on the bay floor and be connected to approach tunnels at either end.

The immersed tube section will be approximately 1.2 kilometres in length, making it one of the longest underwater metro tunnels in Lurandia. The bay floor in this area is composed of soft marine sediments, requiring ground improvement and the use of a gravel bed foundation to prevent settlement.

Two tunnelled sections connect to the immersed tubes: a cut-and-cover approach beneath Original, and a bored underground section linking the tubes to the station box beneath Badia de la Via Verda. The work requires a temporary closure of [[Embarcadero Boulevard (Sant Cristòfor)|Embarcadero Boulevard]] to allow access for excavation and deliveries. Because the Barcelona side of the project is entirely underground and the Original-side works are located beneath commercial property, construction is scheduled to proceed on a 24-hour basis.

=== Badia de la Via Verda modifications ===

At Badia de la Via Verda, the project requires the construction of a new Line 1 station box underneath the existing Line 5 station. The lower level will serve as the terminus for the extended Line 1, with interchange to Line 5 above. The station box is being built with provision for three tracks, allowing for future expansions into Barcelona and a possible Line 2 connection. The construction involves underpinning the existing station box and excavating a new level beneath the current platforms.

The station will feature a new entrance pavilion on the waterfront promenade.

== Cost and funding ==

The project has an original budget of I$550 million, revised to I$680 million following cost overruns associated with the accelerated construction schedule and the complexity of the immersed tube installation. The revised figure represents an approximately 24 per cent increase over the original estimate.

Funding for the project is structured as follows:

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Source !! Amount !! Share
|-
| [[Sant Cristòfor]] municipal bonds || I$420 million || 62%
|-
| [[Estret Province|Estret]] provincial transport grant || I$160 million || 24%
|-
| National government (pending) || I$100 million || 14%
|-
| '''Total''' || '''I$680 million''' || '''100%'''
|}

The Sant Cristòfor Metropolitan Transport Authority issued municipal bonds backed by future fare revenue and property tax increments along the corridor. The bonds were subscribed by domestic financial institutions, including [[Banc Balboa]] and [[Corporació Bancària del Nou Món]] (CBNM). Estret Province approved a transport infrastructure grant from its provincial budget in 2025, covering approximately one-quarter of the project cost.

The national government contribution of I$100 million has not yet been disbursed. The [[Balboan People's Party|BPP]] minority government of President [[Lim Hui-jin]] has indicated support for the project but has not secured Assembly approval for the allocation. The funding request faces opposition from interior-province parties, including the [[Tramuntana Regionalist Party]], which argues that national infrastructure funds should prioritise the suspended [[Sant Cristòfor–Sant Bart high-speed rail|Sant Cristòfor–Sant Bart high-speed rail line]] over a municipal metro extension in the wealthiest city in the country. The [[Socialist Front of Balboa]] (FSB) has urged the government to approve the allocation, citing the project's importance to the Barcelona district and the retraining programme for ferry workers.

As of mid-2026, construction is proceeding on the basis of the municipal and provincial funding, with the national contribution treated as a pending allocation subject to Assembly approval.

== Controversy ==

The Line 1 extension has been the subject of intense debate since its proposal, with opposition centred on its potential impact on the [[Ferries de la Badia de Sant Cristòfor]] and concerns about construction disruption in the densely populated waterfront area.

=== Ferry service impact ===

The Ferries de la Badia de Sant Cristòfor have operated since 1887, providing service between Barcelona and Original across the Badia de Sant Cristòfor. The network carries approximately 118,500 passengers daily in total, with the four Barcelona-peninsula routes together accounting for about 103,900 passengers daily. Route 1 alone carries approximately 68,700 passengers daily. The service employs 340 workers, including 120 ferry operators, 80 maintenance staff, and 140 administrative and support personnel.

Metro officials project that the extension will reduce ferry ridership by 40–60% once fully operational, as passengers switch to the faster metro service. The metro journey from Barcelona to Original will take approximately 8 minutes, compared with 18–22 minutes by ferry. Ferry operators argue that the projections underestimate the continued appeal of the scenic waterfront route and the loyalty of long-standing passengers.

The retraining programme, mandated as a condition of project approval, allocates I$45 million over five years to support ferry workers affected by ridership declines. The programme includes:

* Skills assessment and career counselling for all ferry employees
* Subsidised training for metro operations, maintenance, and customer service roles
* Guaranteed interview schemes with the Metro de Sant Cristòfor for qualified candidates
* Income support for workers in training, equivalent to 80% of previous wages
* Early retirement packages for workers aged 55 and over with 20+ years of service
* Small business grants for workers seeking to transition to entrepreneurship

As of June 2026, approximately 80 ferry workers have enrolled in the programme, with 45 in metro operations training and 35 in maintenance and technical roles. Union officials have criticised the pace of enrolment, arguing that more aggressive outreach is needed to ensure workers are prepared for the transition.

=== Construction disruption ===

Residents and businesses in Original and Barcelona have raised concerns about traffic disruption during the accelerated construction period. The temporary closure of Embarcadero Boulevard has been particularly contentious, although the affected area is commercial and the project has been designed to limit impacts on residences.

The Sant Cristòfor Metropolitan Transport Authority has implemented mitigation measures including:

* 24-hour construction shifts to shorten the build period
* Sound barriers and dust suppression systems at construction sites
* Pedestrian walkways and alternative access routes for affected businesses
* Compensation fund for businesses demonstrating revenue losses exceeding 20%
* Regular community liaison meetings with the project team

Critics argue these measures are insufficient and have called for more substantial compensation and stronger protections for ferry workers and waterfront businesses.

=== Political debate ===

The extension has become a focal point in Balboan transport politics, with proponents arguing it represents necessary modernisation and improved connectivity for the Barcelona district, while opponents characterise it as a wasteful project that prioritises metro expansion over preserving historic transit modes and supporting working-class ferry workers.

Mayor Oriol Cardona i Ousmane has made the extension a signature achievement of his administration, arguing that it will reduce congestion, improve air quality, and provide faster, more reliable service for Barcelona residents. The mayor has emphasised the retraining programme as evidence of the administration's commitment to supporting affected workers.

Opposition parties, including the [[Liberal Party of Balboa]] and the [[Green Alliance (Balboa)|Green Alliance]], have criticised the project's cost, environmental impact, and treatment of ferry workers. The Liberal Party has called for a referendum on the project, arguing that the 2016 vote predates current economic conditions and that residents should have the opportunity to reconsider given cost overruns and changed circumstances. The [[Tramuntana Regionalist Party]] and other interior-province representatives have opposed the pending national government contribution, arguing that national infrastructure funds should prioritise the suspended Sant Cristòfor–Sant Bart high-speed rail line over a municipal metro extension in the wealthiest city in the country.

Labour unions, particularly the [[Sindicat de Treballadors del Port]] and the [[General Union of Balboan Workers]], have been vocal in demanding stronger protections for ferry workers. The unions have threatened industrial action if the retraining programme is not expanded and if guaranteed employment provisions are not strengthened.

Environmental groups have raised concerns about the marine construction, particularly the impact of immersed tube installation on the Badia de Sant Cristòfor ecosystem. The groups have called for independent environmental monitoring and for mitigation measures to protect marine life during construction.

